> I've been playing nethack off and on for a couple of years now, and lurking

This is my second 'on' phase, with a several year gap between 
versions. I first found NetHack on a general "shareware & freeware" 
sampler disk. And yes, I used to save scum way back when [to try 
and help me perform better, it didn't really work]. I don't now.

> in this newsgroup for almost that long, but there is one thing 
> that I am curious about. I know that save scumming is bad, and 
> posting a YAAP that was save scummed is even worse. I'm also 
> aware that the player who can post a YAAP has a great deal of 
> pride in thier accomplishment, and a game that is scummed 
> doesn't have quite the same feeling of success. But when did 
> scumming become bad? 

Always for games designed to suggest it isn't supported. Often in 
games even when there is obvious support for it.

e.g. a friend used 'save scumming' to beat the hardest level (and 
several easier levels) of his chess computer [back in 1985]. The 
feature that allowed the recall of saved positions allowed players 
to try a 'different approach to a given situation'. But would you 
say my friend 'beat' his chess computer?

 
> Every other type of game allows for scumming, and many times they

Many don't. Most arcade-style games don't.

> encouraged it actively. ("It's a good idea to save before using 
> this item") I figure that since nethack can trace it's ancestory 
> to pre-internet days, perhaps there was something in the 
> begining that made scumming bad? Or maybe I am looking at it 
> from the wrong side. When did save scumming become accepted for 
> other games? 

When the save/restore feature became desirable in those games, even 
then I think 'acceptable' is too strong, in general. 

One example where I think 'save scumming' is OK is one my SO finds 
hereslf in in Civilization III from time to time.
That is:
There are many ways to achieve a victory, and she is in the happy 
position of being able to reach two or more of them. The first she 
reaches will end the game.

Typically her choices are between a Diplomatic Victory (be voted-in 
as Secretary General of the UN - the election being between the 
nation that builds it, and the one or two otherwise largest 
nations. The nation that builds it determines if elections will be 
held); and the 'Space Race' victory (first nation to launch a 
rocket for a new planet).

When she is on the verge of completing the UN she'll save her game. 
She'll then complete it and hold elections. Win or lose, she'll 
then load the saved game, complete the UN and _prevent_ elections 
while she strives towards the space race victory.

There's nothing 'morally' wrong with this approach, afaics. 

Repeatedly loading saved points so that she is the one who 
completes the UN first, would, on the other hand, be a Civ III 
version of 'bad' save scumming.

> I'm just curious about the nethack culture versus other games 
> culture. It is really fascinating looking at how people view 
> games, and how they view other players and other games. 

I think the 'morality' of save scumming varies depending on the 
game, and that such is usually readily seen in the structure/design 
of the game. The ultimate 'approval' being a game which gives you 
infinite lives, and the ability to load/save (e.g. Abe's Oddysee on 
the Playstation). The ultimate disapproval being a game that quits 
on save, and deletes save files on death/quit-without-save (e.g. 
NetHack).

